P5.4 — reconnect / partial-loss drill, and a chunk-loss bug it found
specs/readiness-recovery-10-10/tasks.md P5.4

date (UTC): 2026-08-02
commit:     e7d18e8
setup:      real realtime-gateway with REALTIME_CHAOS_DROP_AFTER_CHUNKS=3, MAX_DROPS=2,
            real ml-inference behind it, scripts/chaos-realtime-reconnect.mjs as the client

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RECONNECT: PASS

  connect #1 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 3 chunks -> DROPPED by chaos
  backoff 456ms (equal jitter)
  connect #2 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 3 chunks -> DROPPED by chaos
  backoff 267ms
  connect #3 (fresh single-use ticket) -> 6 chunks -> clean close

  RESULT sent=12/12 acked=12 drops=2 tickets=3

  The reconnect contract holds: a fresh single-use ticket per connect, equal-jitter backoff,
  buffer-and-resume from the right chunk index. The client is told the session survived.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PARTIAL LOSS: FAIL — half the recitation was acknowledged and never stored

  chunks on disk:  6 of 12
  present:         ws-0000 ws-0001 ws-0002 ws-0003 ws-0004 ws-0005
  missing:         ws-0006 .. ws-0011

  Sampled at +0s, +1s, +2s, +5s and +10s with the gateway STILL RUNNING: 6 every time. This is not
  a teardown artifact and it is not a race that settles — the chunks never arrive.

  The chunks were ACCEPTED, not rejected. The client counts only `ack.accepted === true` and
  counted 12, so `forward_chunk` returned Ok for all of them and they entered the session's mpsc
  channel. The forwarding task then delivered a subset and logged nothing about the rest — no
  `ML service rejected`, no retry exhaustion, no backpressure counter.

  ── WHAT THE LOSS ACTUALLY TRACKS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Varying ONLY the forced-drop count, everything else identical:

    drops  connections  stored   chunk ids stored
      0         1        12/12   0000..0011      <- no loss at all
      1         2         9/12   0000..0008
      2         3         6/12   0000..0005

  Two things follow, and the first corrects an earlier reading in this file:

  1. A CLEAN CLOSE LOSES NOTHING. With no drops, one connection delivers all twelve. "The handler
     returns before the detached forwarder drains" was the wrong explanation, and it was wrong in
     the direction that would have sent someone to patch the close path.

  2. The loss is exactly 3 x drops, always at the TAIL of the session, and 3 is
     REALTIME_CHAOS_DROP_AFTER_CHUNKS. So the count is coupled to the dropped connections, but the
     chunks that go missing are the last ones sent on the FINAL connection — not the ones that were
     in flight when the socket died.

  That coupling is the actual thing to explain, and it is not explained yet. Something shared across
  connections is consuming or discarding a fixed quantum per drop. The next step is tracing on the
  forward path and on `try_send`, with the per-connection channel capacity in view — not a fourth
  hypothesis written up as a cause.

  ── on the reliability of this measurement ──
  Three earlier runs said 6/12, 6/12, then 0/12. The 0/12 was a HARNESS ARTIFACT: the gateway had
  panicked at startup (ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS combined with METRICS_DEV_OPEN is refused) and the
  client had connected to a stale gateway from the previous run, still holding the port and
  forwarding into a deleted directory.

  Every run in the table above therefore uses a unique port pair, a fresh mktemp storage dir, and an
  assertion that a gateway OF THAT RUN is listening before anything is measured.

NOT FIXED IN THIS RUN, deliberately. A drain fix on a detached task is the kind of concurrency
change that goes wrong when rushed at the end of a session. The reproduction is deterministic and
takes 20 seconds, so the fix can be written against a red test rather than against a hypothesis.

STILL NOT RUN for P5.4: long-audio, timeout, duplicate-delivery, recovery.
